appened in China. Yet in the seemingly unspectacular events of this ordinary year, Ray Huang finds exemplified the roots of China's perennial inability to adapt to change. Through fascinating accounts of the lives of seven prominent officials, he fashions a remarkably vivid portrayal of the court and the ruling class of late imperial China. In revealing the subtle but inexorable forces that brought about the paralysis and final collapse of the Ming dynasty, Huang offers the reader perspective into the problems China has faced through the centuries.\"-- Provided by publisher"},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"List of illustrations -- Acknowledgments -- Foreword / L. Carrington Goodrich -- 1. The Wan-li emperor -- 2. Shen Shih-hsing, First Grand-Secretary -- 3. A world without Chang Chü-cheng -- 4. The living ancestor -- 5. Hai Jui, the eccentric model official -- 6. Ch'i Chi-kuang, the lonely general -- 7. Li Chih, a divided conscience -- Appendix A : Audience on February 5, 1590 -- Appendix B : Audience on August 25, 1590 -- Notes -- Bibliography -- Index."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Ray Huang."},"\"To be published in Chinese under the title Wanli shiwunian [The fifteenth year of Wan-li] by Zhonghua Shuju, Beijing, People's Republic of China.\"","Includes index.",{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Bibliography: p. 261-265."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"a-cc---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","geographic":"China","topic":"History","temporal":"Ming dynasty, 1368-1644"},{"geographic":"China","topic":"History","temporal":"Ming dynasty, 1368-1644"},{"topic":"China, 1587"},{"@authority":"fast","temporal":"1368-1644"},{"@authority":"swd","temporal":"Geschichte 1573-1619"},{"hierarchicalGeographic":{"country":"United States","state":"Connecticut","city":"New Haven"}}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"DS753 .H798"},{"@authority":"ddc","#text":"951/.026"}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":